<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>potential-potential-difference-and-potential-energy</title></head><body><h1>potential-potential-difference-and-potential-energy</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Potential Difference and Point Charge</li><li>Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, po…</li><li>Potential Difference, Potential Due to a Point Charge, Potential Energy and Electron Volt</li><li>Equipotential Lines and Surfaces</li><li>Equipotential Lines and Surfaces</li><li>Potential Gradient</li></ul><h2>Potential Difference and Point Charge</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Electrostatic Potential & Potential Difference:** Electric potential at a point P is defined as the work done per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge q₀ from infinity to P against the electrostatic force of the source distribution, without altering the field: $V = \frac{W}{q_0} = -\int_\infty^P \vec{E} \cdot d\vec{r}$. Potential difference between two points A and B is $\Delta V = V_B - V_A = -\int_A^B \vec{E} \cdot d\vec{r}$. Both V and ΔV are scalars, have SI unit volt (V), where $1\,\text{V} = 1\,\text{J/C}$, and are path-independent because the electrostatic field is conservative ($\oint \vec{E} \cdot d\vec{r} = 0$).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Potential due to an Isolated Point Charge:** For a source charge Q at the origin, the work done in bringing q₀ from infinity to distance r is $W = -\int_\infty^r \frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 x^2} dx = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} \frac{Q q_0}{r}$. Dividing by q₀ gives the potential: $V(r) = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Q}{r} = k\frac{Q}{r}$. Unlike electric field (which is a vector falling off as 1/r²), potential is a scalar that falls off only as 1/r. For an ensemble of point charges, superposition applies algebraically: $V = k \sum_i \frac{q_i}{r_i}$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Relation between Electric Field and Potential Gradient:** The electric field and potential are intrinsically related. Since $V = -\int \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{r}$, we obtain $\vec{E} = -\vec{\nabla}V$. For a uniform field directed along the x-axis: $E = -\frac{dV}{dx}$. This is the foundational link that converts every electrostatic field formula into a corresponding potential formula, always by substituting $E \propto 1/r^2$ with $V \propto 1/r$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Potential of Standard Charge Distributions:** (1) **Line charge:** $V = -\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\ln(r/r_0)$; (2) **Infinite plane sheet:** $V = -\frac{\sigma}{2\varepsilon_0}x$ (linear decrease, since E is constant); (3) **Charged conducting shell (radius R, charge Q):** $V = kQ/R$ everywhere inside and on the surface, jumping to $V = kQ/r$ outside — a discontinuity of exactly $E R = \sigma R/\varepsilon_0$; (4) **Two parallel plates separated by d:** $E = \frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_0}$ and $V_{AB} = -Ed = -\frac{\sigma d}{\varepsilon_0}$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Potential Energy of a System of Charges:** The potential energy of a charge q brought from infinity into the field of source charge Q is $U = qV = k\frac{qQ}{r}$. For two like charges ($qQ > 0$), U is positive, meaning work must be done against repulsion; for two unlike charges ($qQ < 0$), U is negative, indicating the system is bound and energy is released on assembly. For multiple charges, the total energy is the sum over all distinct pairs: $U = k \sum_{i<j} \frac{q_i q_j}{r_{ij}}$. Energy is a scalar and must never be computed by summing $\vec{F}\cdot d\vec{r}$ naively over individual pairs for conservative systems.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Equivalence of Potential Energy and Force:** By the work-energy theorem, the work done by the electrostatic force in moving a charge slowly from A to B is $W = q(V_A - V_B) = -\Delta U$. Therefore $\Delta U = -W_{electric}$, proving that $\Delta U = -\int \vec{F}\cdot d\vec{r}$. This means a force always acts to reduce potential energy: a positive charge accelerates toward lower V, and a negative charge accelerates toward higher V.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Electron Volt (eV) — Energy Unit Conversion:** The electron volt is the energy gained by a particle of charge e when accelerated through a potential difference of one volt: $1\,\text{eV} = e \times 1\,\text{V} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\,\text{C} \times 1\,\text{J/C} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\,\text{J}$. Convenient conversion factors: $1\,\text{eV} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\,\text{J}$ and $1\,\text{J} = 6.242 \times 10^{18}\,\text{eV}$. Multiples are commonly used: $1\,\text{keV} = 10^3\,\text{eV}$ (X-rays), $1\,\text{MeV} = 10^6\,\text{eV}$ (particle accelerators, nuclear binding), and $1\,\text{GeV} = 10^9\,\text{eV}$ (collider experiments). Note that electron volt is an energy unit, not a charge or potential unit.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Potential V is work done per unit positive charge; SI unit is volt (1 V = 1 J/C).</td></tr><tr><td>Potential is a path-independent scalar; electric field is a vector with direction given by -∇V.</td></tr><tr><td>Potential due to a point charge falls as 1/r, while its electric field falls as 1/r².</td></tr><tr><td>Like charges give positive U (repulsive, unbound); unlike charges give negative U (attractive, bound).</td></tr><tr><td>1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, an energy unit; 1 MeV = 10⁶ eV is standard in nuclear physics.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Treating potential as a vector and adding components: Potential V is a scalar. Only in a region with a clear directional symmetry (e.g. along the axis of a line charge) is it meaningful to write $V = -Ex$; you must never resolve V into vector components like x, y, z components.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing potential difference with potential: Potential difference is a change $\Delta V = V_B - V_A$ between two points and can be positive or negative. Potential V itself is defined relative to infinity ($V_\infty = 0$), so a positive charge gives positive V while an electron gives negative V.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing the electron volt is a unit of charge or potential: The electron volt (eV) is a unit of ENERGY, defined as the energy of a charge of magnitude e accelerated through 1 V. 1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.</td></tr><tr><td>Computing total potential energy by summing over all ordered pairs (i, j) instead of distinct pairs (i < j): Every interaction must be counted exactly once; summing both $q_i q_j$ and $q_j q_i$ double-counts each pair energy.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Electrostatic potential V is the work done per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge from infinity, given by V = -∫E·dr, with SI unit the volt. A point charge Q produces V = kQ/r, falling as 1/r (compared with the field's 1/r²), and fields and potentials are linked by E = -∇V. The potential energy of a charge system is U = kΣqᵢqⱼ/rᵢⱼ, positive for like charges and negative for unlike charges, with ΔU = -W_electric. The electron volt is an energy unit where 1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.</p><hr><h2>Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, po…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Electric potential.** Potential at a point is work done per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge from infinity: V = W/q = (1/4πε₀)q/r for a point charge, measured in volts.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Potential difference.** The potential difference V₂ − V₁ equals the work done per unit charge against the field in moving between the two points; 1 V = 1 J C⁻¹.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Potential energy.** A charge q at potential V has energy U = qV; for two point charges U = q₁q₂/4πε₀r, and for a system the pairwise energies add.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Electron volt.** 1 eV is the energy gained by an electron moving through a potential difference of 1 volt: 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Equipotential surfaces.** These surfaces are everywhere perpendicular to field lines; no work is done moving a charge along one, and the surface of a charged conductor is equipotential.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Potential difference</td></tr><tr><td>• Potential due to a point charge</td></tr><tr><td>• Potential energy</td></tr><tr><td>• Electron volt</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Potential difference" as one blob → ✅ split into: potential difference, potential due to a point charge, potential energy.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, po… (Potential, Potential Difference and Potential Energy, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, potential energy. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Potential Difference, Potential Due to a Point Charge, Potential Energy and Electron Volt</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Electrostatic Potential & Potential Difference:** Electric potential at a point P is defined as the work done per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge q₀ from infinity to P against the electrostatic force of the source distribution, without altering the field: $V = \frac{W}{q_0} = -\int_\infty^P \vec{E} \cdot d\vec{r}$. Potential difference between two points A and B is $\Delta V = V_B - V_A = -\int_A^B \vec{E} \cdot d\vec{r}$. Both V and ΔV are scalars, have SI unit volt (V), where $1\,\text{V} = 1\,\text{J/C}$, and are path-independent because the electrostatic field is conservative ($\oint \vec{E} \cdot d\vec{r} = 0$).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Potential due to an Isolated Point Charge:** For a source charge Q at the origin, the work done in bringing q₀ from infinity to distance r is $W = -\int_\infty^r \frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 x^2} dx = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} \frac{Q q_0}{r}$. Dividing by q₀ gives the potential: $V(r) = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Q}{r} = k\frac{Q}{r}$. Unlike electric field (which is a vector falling off as 1/r²), potential is a scalar that falls off only as 1/r. For an ensemble of point charges, superposition applies algebraically: $V = k \sum_i \frac{q_i}{r_i}$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Relation between Electric Field and Potential Gradient:** The electric field and potential are intrinsically related. Since $V = -\int \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{r}$, we obtain $\vec{E} = -\vec{\nabla}V$. For a uniform field directed along the x-axis: $E = -\frac{dV}{dx}$. This is the foundational link that converts every electrostatic field formula into a corresponding potential formula, always by substituting $E \propto 1/r^2$ with $V \propto 1/r$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Potential of Standard Charge Distributions:** (1) **Line charge:** $V = -\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\ln(r/r_0)$; (2) **Infinite plane sheet:** $V = -\frac{\sigma}{2\varepsilon_0}x$ (linear decrease, since E is constant); (3) **Charged conducting shell (radius R, charge Q):** $V = kQ/R$ everywhere inside and on the surface, jumping to $V = kQ/r$ outside — a discontinuity of exactly $E R = \sigma R/\varepsilon_0$; (4) **Two parallel plates separated by d:** $E = \frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_0}$ and $V_{AB} = -Ed = -\frac{\sigma d}{\varepsilon_0}$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Potential Energy of a System of Charges:** The potential energy of a charge q brought from infinity into the field of source charge Q is $U = qV = k\frac{qQ}{r}$. For two like charges ($qQ > 0$), U is positive, meaning work must be done against repulsion; for two unlike charges ($qQ < 0$), U is negative, indicating the system is bound and energy is released on assembly. For multiple charges, the total energy is the sum over all distinct pairs: $U = k \sum_{i<j} \frac{q_i q_j}{r_{ij}}$. Energy is a scalar and must never be computed by summing $\vec{F}\cdot d\vec{r}$ naively over individual pairs for conservative systems.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Equivalence of Potential Energy and Force:** By the work-energy theorem, the work done by the electrostatic force in moving a charge slowly from A to B is $W = q(V_A - V_B) = -\Delta U$. Therefore $\Delta U = -W_{electric}$, proving that $\Delta U = -\int \vec{F}\cdot d\vec{r}$. This means a force always acts to reduce potential energy: a positive charge accelerates toward lower V, and a negative charge accelerates toward higher V.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Electron Volt (eV) — Energy Unit Conversion:** The electron volt is the energy gained by a particle of charge e when accelerated through a potential difference of one volt: $1\,\text{eV} = e \times 1\,\text{V} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\,\text{C} \times 1\,\text{J/C} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\,\text{J}$. Convenient conversion factors: $1\,\text{eV} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19}\,\text{J}$ and $1\,\text{J} = 6.242 \times 10^{18}\,\text{eV}$. Multiples are commonly used: $1\,\text{keV} = 10^3\,\text{eV}$ (X-rays), $1\,\text{MeV} = 10^6\,\text{eV}$ (particle accelerators, nuclear binding), and $1\,\text{GeV} = 10^9\,\text{eV}$ (collider experiments). Note that electron volt is an energy unit, not a charge or potential unit.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Potential V is work done per unit positive charge; SI unit is volt (1 V = 1 J/C).</td></tr><tr><td>Potential is a path-independent scalar; electric field is a vector with direction given by -∇V.</td></tr><tr><td>Potential due to a point charge falls as 1/r, while its electric field falls as 1/r².</td></tr><tr><td>Like charges give positive U (repulsive, unbound); unlike charges give negative U (attractive, bound).</td></tr><tr><td>1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, an energy unit; 1 MeV = 10⁶ eV is standard in nuclear physics.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Treating potential as a vector and adding components: Potential V is a scalar. Only in a region with a clear directional symmetry (e.g. along the axis of a line charge) is it meaningful to write $V = -Ex$; you must never resolve V into vector components like x, y, z components.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing potential difference with potential: Potential difference is a change $\Delta V = V_B - V_A$ between two points and can be positive or negative. Potential V itself is defined relative to infinity ($V_\infty = 0$), so a positive charge gives positive V while an electron gives negative V.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing the electron volt is a unit of charge or potential: The electron volt (eV) is a unit of ENERGY, defined as the energy of a charge of magnitude e accelerated through 1 V. 1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.</td></tr><tr><td>Computing total potential energy by summing over all ordered pairs (i, j) instead of distinct pairs (i < j): Every interaction must be counted exactly once; summing both $q_i q_j$ and $q_j q_i$ double-counts each pair energy.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Electrostatic potential V is the work done per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge from infinity, given by V = -∫E·dr, with SI unit the volt. A point charge Q produces V = kQ/r, falling as 1/r (compared with the field's 1/r²), and fields and potentials are linked by E = -∇V. The potential energy of a charge system is U = kΣqᵢqⱼ/rᵢⱼ, positive for like charges and negative for unlike charges, with ΔU = -W_electric. The electron volt is an energy unit where 1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.</p><hr><h2>Equipotential Lines and Surfaces</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition of an Equipotential Surface:** An equipotential surface (or equipotential line in two dimensions) is a geometrical surface on which the electric potential V is the same at every point. Since $\Delta V = 0$ between any two points on it, no work is done by the electrostatic field in moving a charge anywhere along the surface: $W = q\Delta V = 0$. Every equipotential surface is a level surface of the scalar potential field, just as contour lines on a topographic map join points of equal height.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Fundamental Properties of Equipotential Surfaces:** (1) **No work is done** on a charge moving along the surface ($W = q\Delta V = 0$); (2) **They are always perpendicular to electric field lines** wherever the field is non-zero; (3) **They never intersect one another** — two intersecting surfaces would assign two different potentials to the same point; (4) **The spacing between successive equipotential surfaces indicates field strength** — closely spaced surfaces signify a strong electric field and rapidly changing potential; (5) **A conductor in electrostatic equilibrium is a single equipotential surface**, since free charges redistribute until the internal field vanishes.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Proof that Equipotential Surfaces are Perpendicular to Field Lines:** Let A and B be two points a distance $\Delta s$ apart on the same equipotential surface, so $\Delta V = 0$. The change in potential along a displacement is $\Delta V = -\vec{E} \cdot \Delta\vec{s} = -E\,\Delta s\cos\theta$. Since $\Delta V = 0$, we must have $\cos\theta = 0$, meaning $\theta = 90^\circ$. Therefore the displacement $\Delta\vec{s}$ (which lies along the equipotential surface) is always perpendicular to $\vec{E}$, confirming that equipotential surfaces and field lines intersect at right angles everywhere.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Equipotential Surfaces for Standard Charge Configurations:** (1) **Isolated point charge Q:** $V = kQ/r$ is constant on a sphere of radius r, so equipotentials are concentric spheres, and the normal to each sphere is radial, exactly along the field lines. (2) **Infinite line charge λ:** $V = -\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\ln\frac{r}{r_0}$, so equipotentials are coaxial cylinders of radius r, with field lines radial and perpendicular. (3) **Infinite plane sheet with surface density σ:** $V = -\frac{\sigma}{2\varepsilon_0}x$, so equipotentials are infinite planes parallel to the sheet, uniformly spaced since E is constant. (4) **Two parallel plates:** planes parallel to the plates, densely packed between the plates (where E is large) and widely spaced outside (where E is zero).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Equipotential Surface of a Conducting Sphere:** For a conducting sphere of radius R carrying charge Q, the entire surface of the sphere is at a single potential $V = kQ/R$. Taking the outside potential, $V(r) = kQ/r$, the equipotentials are concentric spheres of radius r ≥ R, all larger than the sphere itself. Inside the hollow conductor, V is constant at $kQ/R$ for every point $r < R$, so the entire interior cavity constitutes one equipotential region. Because E = 0 inside, there is no field direction to define, and the conductor plus its interior forms a single continuous equipotential volume.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Work and Energy Relations for Equipotential Surfaces:** Moving a charge q from equipotential surface V₁ to V₂ requires external work $W_{ext} = q\Delta V = q(V_2 - V_1)$ and changes the system's potential energy by exactly the same amount: $\Delta U = q\Delta V$. For a positive charge, work is required to move it to a higher potential; for an electron (negative charge), it is spontaneous. Two equipotential surfaces at potentials differing by 1 volt, for a single electron, differ in potential energy by exactly 1 eV — this is precisely why the electron volt is the natural energy unit when discussing potential differences.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>No work is done by the electric field in moving a charge along an equipotential surface (W = qΔV = 0).</td></tr><tr><td>Equipotential surfaces are always perpendicular to electric field lines.</td></tr><tr><td>Two equipotential surfaces can never intersect one another.</td></tr><tr><td>Closely spaced equipotential surfaces indicate a strong electric field; widely spaced ones indicate a weak field.</td></tr><tr><td>A conductor in electrostatic equilibrium is a single equipotential surface at constant V = kQ/R.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Mistaking equipotential surfaces for field lines: Field lines show the direction of the force on a positive test charge; equipotential surfaces join points of equal potential. The two families are always mutually perpendicular, never parallel.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing that equipotential surfaces are widely spaced where the field is weak: Spacing is inversely related to field strength. Close spacing means a large potential change over a short distance, i.e. a strong field (E = -ΔV/Δs).</td></tr><tr><td>Assuming the potential on the surface of a charged conductor varies with position: In electrostatic equilibrium, free charges redistribute until the interior field is zero, making the entire conductor surface a single equipotential at V = kQ/R.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> An equipotential surface is a locus of points at identical potential V, so no work is done by the field along it (W = qΔV = 0). Equipotential surfaces can never intersect and are always perpendicular to electric field lines, since dV = -E·dl = 0 forces the displacement along the surface to be normal to E. Their spacing indicates field strength: close spacing means a strong field. For a point charge they are concentric spheres, for a line charge coaxial cylinders, and for an infinite plane sheet parallel planes.</p><hr><h2>Equipotential Lines and Surfaces</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition of an Equipotential Surface:** An equipotential surface (or equipotential line in two dimensions) is a geometrical surface on which the electric potential V is the same at every point. Since $\Delta V = 0$ between any two points on it, no work is done by the electrostatic field in moving a charge anywhere along the surface: $W = q\Delta V = 0$. Every equipotential surface is a level surface of the scalar potential field, just as contour lines on a topographic map join points of equal height.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Fundamental Properties of Equipotential Surfaces:** (1) **No work is done** on a charge moving along the surface ($W = q\Delta V = 0$); (2) **They are always perpendicular to electric field lines** wherever the field is non-zero; (3) **They never intersect one another** — two intersecting surfaces would assign two different potentials to the same point; (4) **The spacing between successive equipotential surfaces indicates field strength** — closely spaced surfaces signify a strong electric field and rapidly changing potential; (5) **A conductor in electrostatic equilibrium is a single equipotential surface**, since free charges redistribute until the internal field vanishes.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Proof that Equipotential Surfaces are Perpendicular to Field Lines:** Let A and B be two points a distance $\Delta s$ apart on the same equipotential surface, so $\Delta V = 0$. The change in potential along a displacement is $\Delta V = -\vec{E} \cdot \Delta\vec{s} = -E\,\Delta s\cos\theta$. Since $\Delta V = 0$, we must have $\cos\theta = 0$, meaning $\theta = 90^\circ$. Therefore the displacement $\Delta\vec{s}$ (which lies along the equipotential surface) is always perpendicular to $\vec{E}$, confirming that equipotential surfaces and field lines intersect at right angles everywhere.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Equipotential Surfaces for Standard Charge Configurations:** (1) **Isolated point charge Q:** $V = kQ/r$ is constant on a sphere of radius r, so equipotentials are concentric spheres, and the normal to each sphere is radial, exactly along the field lines. (2) **Infinite line charge λ:** $V = -\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\ln\frac{r}{r_0}$, so equipotentials are coaxial cylinders of radius r, with field lines radial and perpendicular. (3) **Infinite plane sheet with surface density σ:** $V = -\frac{\sigma}{2\varepsilon_0}x$, so equipotentials are infinite planes parallel to the sheet, uniformly spaced since E is constant. (4) **Two parallel plates:** planes parallel to the plates, densely packed between the plates (where E is large) and widely spaced outside (where E is zero).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Equipotential Surface of a Conducting Sphere:** For a conducting sphere of radius R carrying charge Q, the entire surface of the sphere is at a single potential $V = kQ/R$. Taking the outside potential, $V(r) = kQ/r$, the equipotentials are concentric spheres of radius r ≥ R, all larger than the sphere itself. Inside the hollow conductor, V is constant at $kQ/R$ for every point $r < R$, so the entire interior cavity constitutes one equipotential region. Because E = 0 inside, there is no field direction to define, and the conductor plus its interior forms a single continuous equipotential volume.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Work and Energy Relations for Equipotential Surfaces:** Moving a charge q from equipotential surface V₁ to V₂ requires external work $W_{ext} = q\Delta V = q(V_2 - V_1)$ and changes the system's potential energy by exactly the same amount: $\Delta U = q\Delta V$. For a positive charge, work is required to move it to a higher potential; for an electron (negative charge), it is spontaneous. Two equipotential surfaces at potentials differing by 1 volt, for a single electron, differ in potential energy by exactly 1 eV — this is precisely why the electron volt is the natural energy unit when discussing potential differences.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>No work is done by the electric field in moving a charge along an equipotential surface (W = qΔV = 0).</td></tr><tr><td>Equipotential surfaces are always perpendicular to electric field lines.</td></tr><tr><td>Two equipotential surfaces can never intersect one another.</td></tr><tr><td>Closely spaced equipotential surfaces indicate a strong electric field; widely spaced ones indicate a weak field.</td></tr><tr><td>A conductor in electrostatic equilibrium is a single equipotential surface at constant V = kQ/R.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Mistaking equipotential surfaces for field lines: Field lines show the direction of the force on a positive test charge; equipotential surfaces join points of equal potential. The two families are always mutually perpendicular, never parallel.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing that equipotential surfaces are widely spaced where the field is weak: Spacing is inversely related to field strength. Close spacing means a large potential change over a short distance, i.e. a strong field (E = -ΔV/Δs).</td></tr><tr><td>Assuming the potential on the surface of a charged conductor varies with position: In electrostatic equilibrium, free charges redistribute until the interior field is zero, making the entire conductor surface a single equipotential at V = kQ/R.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> An equipotential surface is a locus of points at identical potential V, so no work is done by the field along it (W = qΔV = 0). Equipotential surfaces can never intersect and are always perpendicular to electric field lines, since dV = -E·dl = 0 forces the displacement along the surface to be normal to E. Their spacing indicates field strength: close spacing means a strong field. For a point charge they are concentric spheres, for a line charge coaxial cylinders, and for an infinite plane sheet parallel planes.</p><hr><h2>Potential Gradient</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition of Potential Gradient:** The potential gradient at a point is the rate of change of electric potential with distance measured in a specified direction, in the limit as the displacement approaches zero. Mathematically, for a uniform field along the x-axis, $E = -\frac{dV}{dx}$. More generally, the magnitude of the potential gradient equals the magnitude of the electric field: $|\vec{E}| = |\nabla V|$. The SI unit of potential gradient is volt per metre (V/m), which is dimensionally identical to N/C since 1 V/m = 1 (J/C)/m = 1 N/C.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Vector Form of Potential Gradient:** The complete relationship between field and potential is $\vec{E} = -\vec{\nabla}V$, where $\vec{\nabla} = \hat{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial x} + \hat{j}\frac{\partial}{\partial y} + \hat{k}\frac{\partial}{\partial z}$ is the gradient (del) operator. The negative sign is physically essential: the electric field always points in the direction of steepest decrease of potential, exactly as a ball rolls downhill. Written in Cartesian components: $E_x = -\frac{\partial V}{\partial x}$, $E_y = -\frac{\partial V}{\partial y}$, $E_z = -\frac{\partial V}{\partial z}$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Gradient for Standard Charge Distributions:** (1) **Point charge:** $V = kQ/r \Rightarrow E = -\frac{d}{dr}\left(\frac{kQ}{r}\right) = \frac{kQ}{r^2}$, recovering the inverse-square law exactly; (2) **Infinite line charge:** $V = -\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\ln\frac{r}{r_0} \Rightarrow E = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0 r}$, recovering the 1/r dependence; (3) **Infinite plane sheet:** $V = -\frac{\sigma}{2\varepsilon_0}x \Rightarrow E = \frac{\sigma}{2\varepsilon_0}$, a constant gradient matching the uniform field; (4) **Charged spherical shell (outside):** $V = \frac{kQ}{r} \Rightarrow E = \frac{kQ}{r^2}$, identical to a point charge at the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Graphical Interpretation of Potential Gradient:** A graph of potential V against distance r along a field line has slope equal to minus the potential gradient. The graph is a straight line for a uniform field, a hyperbola for a point charge or dipole, and a logarithmic curve for a line charge. Where the graph is steep, the field is strong; where it is flat, the field is weak. At the point of inflection in the potential curve of a point charge's neighbourhood, the gradient and hence the field attain their maximum value.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Practical Determination of Potential Gradient:** The potential gradient along a conductor or a wire carrying a steady current is measured by the potential drop per unit length, $K = \frac{V}{l}$, and equals the electric field E in that conductor. In a uniform current-carrying wire of length l and cross-sectional area A carrying current I and having resistivity ρ, $K = \frac{IR}{\text{Area}} = \frac{I\rho}{A}$, a result obtained by combining Ohm's law with $E = V/l$. This is the standard method for determining the resistivity of a material, and it is also the basis of the potentiometric and Kelvin bridge methods of measurement.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Potential gradient is the rate of change of potential with distance, measured in V/m (identical to N/C).</td></tr><tr><td>Electric field equals the negative of the potential gradient: E = -dV/dx.</td></tr><tr><td>The field always points in the direction of steepest decrease of potential.</td></tr><tr><td>Differentiating V = kQ/r reproduces the inverse-square law E = kQ/r² exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>Slope of the potential–distance graph is minus the electric field at that point.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Omitting the negative sign in E = -dV/dx: The minus sign indicates that the field points toward decreasing potential. Writing E = dV/dx gives the wrong direction whenever V decreases along the chosen axis.</td></tr><tr><td>Assuming the potential gradient is a vector while differentiating only with respect to one variable: For a general three-dimensional field the gradient is a vector; the scalar expression dV/dx is valid only along a chosen axis where the field is known to be uniform or one-dimensional.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing the potential gradient with the gradient of a scalar function in general mathematics: In electrostatics the potential gradient carries a minus sign and is identical in magnitude to the electric field, but a generic mathematical gradient is always positive in magnitude and has no such sign convention.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The potential gradient is the rate of change of potential with distance, K = dV/dl, and equals the electric field in magnitude with opposite sign: E = -dV/dx or in vector form E = -∇V. The negative sign shows the field always points toward decreasing potential. Differentiating the standard potentials (point charge kQ/r, line charge -λ/2πε₀ ln r, plane sheet -σx/2ε₀) recovers the corresponding field expressions exactly. Practically, the potential gradient along a current-carrying wire equals Iρ/A and is the basis of resistivity measurement.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Potential Difference and Point Charge</td><td>Electrostatic potential V is the work done per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge from infinity, given by V = -∫E·dr, with SI unit the volt. A point charge Q produces V = kQ/r, falling as 1/r (compared with the field's 1/r²), and fields and potentials are linked by E = -∇V. The potential energy of a charge system is U = kΣqᵢqⱼ/rᵢⱼ, positive for like charges and negative for unlike charges, with ΔU = -W_electric. The electron volt is an energy unit where 1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, po…</td><td>Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, po… (Potential, Potential Difference and Potential Energy, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, potential energy. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Potential Difference, Potential Due to a Point Charge, Potential Energy and Electron Volt</td><td>Electrostatic potential V is the work done per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge from infinity, given by V = -∫E·dr, with SI unit the volt. A point charge Q produces V = kQ/r, falling as 1/r (compared with the field's 1/r²), and fields and potentials are linked by E = -∇V. The potential energy of a charge system is U = kΣqᵢqⱼ/rᵢⱼ, positive for like charges and negative for unlike charges, with ΔU = -W_electric. The electron volt is an energy unit where 1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Equipotential Lines and Surfaces</td><td>An equipotential surface is a locus of points at identical potential V, so no work is done by the field along it (W = qΔV = 0). Equipotential surfaces can never intersect and are always perpendicular to electric field lines, since dV = -E·dl = 0 forces the displacement along the surface to be normal to E. Their spacing indicates field strength: close spacing means a strong field. For a point charge they are concentric spheres, for a line charge coaxial cylinders, and for an infinite plane sheet parallel planes.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Equipotential Lines and Surfaces</td><td>An equipotential surface is a locus of points at identical potential V, so no work is done by the field along it (W = qΔV = 0). Equipotential surfaces can never intersect and are always perpendicular to electric field lines, since dV = -E·dl = 0 forces the displacement along the surface to be normal to E. Their spacing indicates field strength: close spacing means a strong field. For a point charge they are concentric spheres, for a line charge coaxial cylinders, and for an infinite plane sheet parallel planes.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Potential Gradient</td><td>The potential gradient is the rate of change of potential with distance, K = dV/dl, and equals the electric field in magnitude with opposite sign: E = -dV/dx or in vector form E = -∇V. The negative sign shows the field always points toward decreasing potential. Differentiating the standard potentials (point charge kQ/r, line charge -λ/2πε₀ ln r, plane sheet -σx/2ε₀) recovers the corresponding field expressions exactly. Practically, the potential gradient along a current-carrying wire equals Iρ/A and is the basis of resistivity measurement.</td></tr></table></body></html>